    "q-124569": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124569",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14134323/assets/deff964a1833339681fc38b30e32ebd3081e7ba1c4b5aabd0f4998f60a2aa78c.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,\\frac{v-1}{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{1-v}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14134323/assets/daefc9e14775a955e5210f965e474c06bc7cac1df9306941b73190cc8bf62219.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-3,-2),(-1,2),$ and $(0,2).$ \n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14134323/assets/94a2a90dae43d1edb1f0154a8c7ac60b8c35352fd72d5193014b9bba204d192f.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-1<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving.",
